touch upon; mention.—'''3†.''' To convict; condemn. Compare ''attaint'', ''v.'', 3-5.—'''4.''' To come so near as to touch; reach, achieve, or accomplish (an end or object) by continued effort; come into possession of; acquire; gain.
{{smaller block|Ends we seek we never shall ''attain''.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Self-Deception.}}}}
'''5.''' To come to or arrive at (a place); reach (a place, time, or state).
{{smaller block|Canaan he now ''attains''. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 135.}}
He has scarce ''attained'' the age of thirty.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, iii.}}}}
'''6.''' To reach in excellence or degree; equal.
{{smaller block|So the first precedent, if it be good, is seldom ''attained'' by imitation. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
'''7†.''' To overtake; come up with: as, “not ''attaining'' him in time,” ''Bacon''.—'''8†.''' To come to know; experience. ''Chaucer.'' ='''Syn. 4.''' {{smaller|''Attain'', ''Obtain'', ''Procure'', reach, achieve, get possession of, carry. (See lists under ''acquire'' and ''accomplish''; also note under ''attainable''.) ''Attain'' involves the idea of considerable effort, while ''obtain'' does not necessarily imply effort at all, and ''procure'' only a small degree of it: thus, we may ''obtain'' property by inheritance, we may ''procure'' a book by purchase, but we can ''attain'' an end only by exertion. ''Attain'' generally has higher or more abstract objects than ''obtain'' or ''procure'': as, to ''obtain'' an office or a patent; to ''procure'' a chair; to ''attain'' eminence; ''attain'' one’s end. In these cases it would be ludicrous to use ''attain'' in place of ''obtain'' or ''procure''.}}
{{smaller block|The Khans, or story-tellers in Ispahan, ''attain'' a controlling power over their audience, keeping them for many hours attentive to the most fanciful and extravagant adventures. {{right|''Emerson'', Eloquence.}}
<poem>Some pray for riches; riches they ''obtain'';
But, watch’d by robbers, for their wealth are slain.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., l. 424.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Have you a catalogue
Of all the voices that we have ''procur’d'',
Set down by the poll?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iii. 3.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To reach; come or arrive by motion, bodily or mental exertion, or efforts of any kind: followed by ''to'' or ''unto''.
{{smaller block|Such knowledge is too wonderful for me; it is high; I can not ''attain unto'' it.
{{right|Ps. cxxxix. 6.}}}}
'''2†.''' To pertain; have relation. ''Chaucer.''

'''attaint†''' (ạ-tān′), ''n.'' [< ''attain'', ''v.''] Something attained. ''Glanville.''

'''attainability''' (ạ-tā-nạ-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''attainable'': see ''-bility''.] Attainableness. ''Coleridge.''

'''attainable''' (ạ-tā′nạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ''attain'' + ''-able.''] Capable of being attained.
{{smaller block|He [Plato] quits the normal for the ''attainable.''
{{right|''De Quincey'', Plato.}}
All that is said of the wise man by Stoic, or oriental or modern essayist, describes to each reader his own idea, his unattained but ''attainable'' self.
{{right|''Emerson'', History.}}}}
{{smaller block|[''Attainable'' was formerly sometimes used where ''obtainable'' or ''procurable'' would now be preferred, as in the following passages:}}
{{smaller block|The kind and quality of food and liquor, the species of habitation, furniture, and clothing, to which the common people of each country are habituated, must be ''attainable'' with ease and certainty.
{{right|''Paley.''}}
General Howe would not permit them [clothes and blankets] to be purchased in Philadelphia, and they were not ''attainable'' in the country.
{{right|''Marshall'', Life of Washington.]}}}}
='''Syn.''' Practicable, feasible, possible, within reach.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|The ''attainment'' of every desired object.
{{right|''Sir W. Jones'', Hitópadésa.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is attained, or obtained by exertion; acquisition; acquirement.
{{smaller block|Formerly the natural impulse of every man was, spontaneously to use the language of life; the language of books was a secondary ''attainment'' not made without effort.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Style, i.}}}}
{{smaller block|Smatterers, whose ''attainments'' just suffice to elevate them from the insignificance of dunces to the dignity of bores.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Mill on Government.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 2.''' ''Acquirements'', ''Acquisitions'', etc. See ''acquirement''.}}

'''attainor†''', ''n.'' [< ''attain'' + ''-or'', after AF. ''atteignour''.] One of the jurors in the process called ''attaint'' (which see).

'''attaint''' (a̤-tānt′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''ataynten'', ''ateynten'', ''atteinten'', etc., an inf. due to ''ataynt'', ''atteint'', pp. of ''ataynen'', etc., after OF. ''ateint'', pp. of ''ateindre'': see ''attain'' and ''attainder''. Later erroneously associated with ''taint'', stain, corruption, to which some of the senses are due.] '''1†.''' To touch; hit in tilting.—'''2†.''' To attain; ascertain.—'''3†.''' To convict (a jury) of having given a false verdict.—'''4.''' To affect with attainder; pass judgment on, as on one found guilty of a crime, as felony or treason, involving forfeiture of civil privileges.
{{smaller block|I dare undertake, that at this day there are more ''attaynted'' landes, concealed from her Majestie, then she hath now possessions in all Ireland.
{{right|''Spenser'', Present State of Ireland.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I must offend before I be ''attainted''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., ii. 4.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|No attainder of treason shall work corruption of blood, or forfeiture except during the life of the person ''attainted''.
{{right|''Constitution of U. S.'', iii. 3.}}}}
'''5†.''' To accuse: with ''of'': as, to ''attaint'' a person ''of'' sorcery.
{{smaller block|He was ''attainted'' . . . ''of'' high treason.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Bolingbroke.}}}}
'''6†.''' To affect with any passion or emotion.
{{smaller block|This noble woman . . . ''attainted'' with extreme sorrow.
{{right|''Historia Anglica'' (trans.).}}}}
'''7.''' To taint; disgrace; cloud with infamy; stain; corrupt.
{{smaller block|Lest she with blame her honour should ''attaint''.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. i. 5.}}
That the pleasure is of an inferior order, can no more ''attaint'' the idea or model of the composition, than it can impeach the excellence of an epigram that it is not a tragedy.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Rhetoric.}}}}

'''attaint†''' (a-tānt′), ''p. a.'' [The older pp. of ''attaint'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Attainted; convicted.—'''2.''' Tainted; corrupted; infected; attacked.
{{smaller block|<poem>My tender youth was never yet ''attaint''
With any passion.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 5.}}}}
'''Auterfoits attaint.''' {{smaller|See ''auterfoits''.}}

'''attaint''' (a-tānt′), ''n.'' [< ''attaint'', ''v.''] '''1†.''' The act of touching or hitting; specifically, in ''tilting'', a hit. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|“You, reverend sir,” said the knight, “have in the encounter of our wits made a fair ''attaint''.”
{{right|''Scott'', Monastery, I. xvi.}}}}
'''2.''' A blow or wound on the leg of a horse caused by overreaching.—'''3.''' An ancient legal process

readings.c3s1:
instituted for reversing a false verdict given by a jury; conviction of a jury for giving such a verdict.—'''4.''' In ''old law:'' (''a'') A conviction. (''b'') Impeachment.—'''5†.''' Infection; injurious or deleterious action.
{{smaller block|The marrow-eating sickness, whose ''attaint''<br>Disorder breeds. {{right|''Shak.'', V. and A., l. 741.}}}}
'''6.''' Attainder.
{{smaller block|It was a point of honour with his [Bismarck’s] Government that the captive royalists should at every cost almost be set free, without ''attaint'' of life or fortune.
{{right|''Lowe'', Bismarck, I. 220.}}}}
'''7†.''' A stain, spot, or taint; hence, a disgrace; an imputation involving dishonor.
{{smaller block|What simple thief brags of his own ''attaint?''
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iii. 2.}}}}

'''attaintment†''' (ạ-tānt′me̤nt), ''n.'' [< ''attaint'' + ''-ment.''] The act or state of being attainted or affected with attainder; conviction; arrest; impeachment.
{{smaller block|When this man was attainted there, and they had liberty to say nay to his ''attaintment'' if they would, sure I am the most allowed it, and else it would not have gone forward. ''Latimer'', quoted in Dixon’s Hist. Church of Eng., xv.}}

'''attainture†''' (ạ-tān′tṳ̄r), ''n.'' [< ''attaint'' + ''-ure.''] '''1.''' Same as ''attaintment.''
{{smaller block|Her ''attainture'' will be Humphrey’s fall.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., i. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' Imputation; stain.
{{smaller block|Without the least ''attainture'' of your valour.
{{right|''Chapman'', Byron’s Tragedy, iii. 1.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''attaminate†''' (a-tam′i-nāt), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''attaminatus'', pp. of ''attaminare'', contaminate: see ''attame''<sup>2</sup>.] To contaminate. ''Blount.''

'''attap''' (at′ap), ''n.'' [Native name; also written ''atap'' and ''adap''.] The ''Nipa fruticans'', a nearly stemless palm of the tidal forests of the East Indian archipelago. {{smaller|Its smooth pinnate leaves are from 15 to 30 feet long, very thick and strong, and are extensively used for thatching.}}
{{smaller block|The roof is thatched with the common Buttam ''attaps'' in the same way as Malay houses.
{{right|''Jour. Anthrop. Inst.'', XV. 293.}}}}

'''attaque†''', ''v.'' and ''n.'' A former spelling of ''attack''.

'''attar''' (at′är), ''n.'' [Also written ''atar'', ''ottar'', and ''otto''; < Pers. ''‘atar'', Hind. ''atr'', < Ar. ''‘itr'', fragrance, perfume, esp. of roses (Pers. ''‘atar-gūl'', attar of roses), < ''‘atara'', smell sweet.] In the East Indies, a general term for a perfume from flowers. {{smaller|In Europe it generally denotes only the ''attar'' or ''otto of roses'', an essential oil made in Turkey and various other eastern countries, chiefly from the damask rose, ''Rosa Damascena''. The yield is very small, 150 pounds of rose-leaves yielding less than an ounce of attar. The principal source of the attar of commerce is in the vicinity of Kazanlik, on the southern side of the Balkan mountains, in Eastern Rumelia. The pure oil solidifies at a temperature between 60° and 65° F. It is a well-known perfume, but the odor is agreeable only when diffused, being too powerful when it is concentrated. It is largely used in the scenting of snuff. Also written ''ottar'', ''otto''.}}

'''attask†''' (a-tȧsk′), ''v. t.'' [< ''at-'' + ''task''.] To task; tax; reprove; blame.
{{smaller block|<poem>You are much more ''attask’d'' for want of wisdom,
Than prais’d for harmful mildness.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 4.}}}}
